WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.532
Certificates support identity validation by binding a name or identity to
a public key under a trust model.

2
00:00:05.532 --> 00:00:10.511
The matching private key must remain protected by the service or subject
that owns the identity.

3
00:00:10.511 --> 00:00:19.794
When a Python client connects to a service, it can examine the certificate
presented by that service and decide whether the identity and trust chain
match what the client expected.

4
00:00:19.794 --> 00:00:24.219
Certificate authorities and certificate chains make that trust decision
scalable.

5
00:00:24.219 --> 00:00:31.104
A certificate chain connects an endpoint certificate through one or more
intermediate certificates to a trusted root certificate.

6
00:00:31.104 --> 00:00:35.407
Trust stores contain roots or authorities that the client accepts for
validation.

7
00:00:35.407 --> 00:00:45.365
Public certificate authorities, private certificate authorities, and self-
signed certificates can all appear in real systems, but each has different
operational and trust implications.

8
00:00:45.365 --> 00:00:46.349
Names matter.

9
00:00:46.349 --> 00:00:52.189
Certificates contain identity information such as subject names and
subject alternative names at a high level.

10
00:00:52.189 --> 00:00:58.643
The client must compare the intended service name with the names in the
certificate according to expected validation rules.

11
00:00:58.643 --> 00:01:02.761
Expiration also matters because certificates are issued for a limited
period.

12
00:01:02.761 --> 00:01:07.802
A certificate that was valid yesterday may fail tomorrow if renewal is not
planned and monitored.

13
00:01:07.802 --> 00:01:09.646
Private key protection is central.

14
00:01:09.646 --> 00:01:16.285
If a private key is exposed, the trust in the associated service identity
may be undermined depending on the system and exposure.

15
00:01:16.285 --> 00:01:23.231
Keys should not be committed to source code, copied into broad logs,
stored in shared folders, or handled casually in support bundles.

16
00:01:23.231 --> 00:01:26.981
Certificate management is not only about getting a certificate issued.

17
00:01:26.981 --> 00:01:31.960
It is about protecting the private key, documenting ownership, and
managing lifecycle safely.
